belarusian leader alexander lukashenko  and russian president vladimir putin  have decided to deepen economic ties  between their two nations during their  recent meeting they agreed on forming  common energy and financial markets now  the move comes as belarus faces western  sanctions in response to political  repression in the country the two  countries have also started joint  military exercises that are being  closely monitored by nato but many bella  russians don't want their country to be  reliant on russia and have criticized  moscow's growing dominance  i'd like to ask  the critics of our integration  with russia  where do you see a burden for russia  there's nothing bad in these programs  for the russian  and belarusian  for the past several years  a lot of work has been carried out on a  package of documents to further deepen  the integration process  between russia and belarus  we're talking about 28 so-called union  programs which are aimed at unifying the  legislation of russia and belarus and  various areas of the economy  and for more on this i'm joined now by  our correspondent in moscow emily  sherwin emily thanks for joining us um  the creation of an alliance of states is  something that's been discussed for  around 20 years so let's talk about  timing why is it taking shape right now  well absolutely this has a long history  um the two countries signed a document  in 1999 that actually created a union  state and which basically allows for the  two countries to almost become uh one  country uh with you know a common  parliament and a common currency and now  in the past few years since 2019  especially the russian side has been  pushing um for a part of that document  at least to be carried out in this new  road map these 28 points that putin was  talking about um just now  before lukashenko  was very resilient very resistant very  um  you know loath to sign anything and has  been kind of there's been a lot of  pushback from the belarusian side um but  now he agreed and i think at least in  part uh that's because lukashenko has  kind of backed into a corner at the  moment before he um his policy for the  last few years had been to kind of  position himself or position belarus  between the west and russia but now he's  under eu and u.s sanctions after the  crackdown on opposition protests last  year and it's really only russia that  has been backing him and propping him up  including uh with loans um and i think  that's part of why we're seeing this uh  this step now kind of as a concession to  russia  now in belarus critics of these closer  ties with russia are worried that  they're going to lead to a loss of their  nation's independence it sounds like  from what you're saying they're right to  be fearful about that  i think anyone who was fearful about  that before will be even more fearful  now uh yesterday during their press  conference about the agreement both  putin and lukashenko said that these  closer economic ties were a first step  that they could potentially provide a  foundation or a basis for moving on to  closer political ties as well even  though both leaders stressed that they  hadn't yet discussed that kind of a  development now  this integration has been an unpopular  measure in belarus the last few times i  was there i always asked people about it  and they said that they were worried  that russia could swallow up uh belarus  and lukashenko seems aware of that  yesterday he he talked about the fact  that that was definitely not going to  happen and that that belarus and russia  are equal partners um in this agreement  but i think people who are scared will  probably continue being scared we'll  have to watch this space essentially  emily sherwin in moscow thanks so much  you
 

